  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by targeting improved upper bounds through evolutionary optimization. The bottleneck of previous approaches was local optima trapping in symmetric structures. Our approach uses genetic programming with adaptive crossover probabilities and a novel multi-stage repair mechanism. The core idea is to explore asymmetric step function structures that can yield smaller overlaps by strategically positioning and timing the transitions in the function. This allows breaking away from symmetric parent solutions and discovering more efficient overlap configurations.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nOur representation uses step functions parameterized by positions and heights. The objective is to minimize:\n\n    max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nwith constraints:\n    0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n    \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)dx = 0.5\n\nWe discretize [0,2] with n=280 samples. Our candidates are represented by pairs (\u03b8, \u03b1) where \u03b8 defines positions and \u03b1 defines heights. The fitness function is computed using the correlation-based overlap integral from np.correlate after integral/clip repair.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Initialize a population of candidate functions derived from parent patterns (if available)\n- Implement tournament selection with three-stage tournament\n- Use combined single- and uniform-crossover with adaptive rate\n- Apply polynomial mutation at stage transitions\n- Maintain memory of best candidates from previous generations\n- Use multi-modal repair: automatic when scale is impossible, clipping-based when feasible\n- Include restart penalizing ratio\n- Ensure proper parent reconstruction from global repository\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Population size: 150\n- Tournament selection size: 3\n- Crossover rates: [.7, .3] for early/late stages\n- Mutation rates: [.01, .005, .001] for different regions\n- Repair strategies: Progressive clip-scales (PPC), direct (DR), adaptive (AR)\n- Critical f-string for repair scaling\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Run for 120 iterations with 150 candidates\n- Use non-stop execution tracking\n- Repair any invalid candidates via reconstruction from parent\n- Resource estimates: negligible memory, focus on convergence\n- Operation sequence: construct/evaluate/select/mutate/repair\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Return triple: (final_candidate, computed_c5, n_points=280)\n- Always enforce 0\u2264x\u22641 and integral=0.25\n- Handle parent reconstruction if repair fails\n- Throw no exceptions, as parent is always available via global variable",
